(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-spinal-cord-sensory-quiz"]={"title":"Spinal Cord & Sensory Receptors — Anatomy Exam 1","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How long is the spinal cord, and to what vertebral level does it extend?","choices":["20 cm; extends from the brain to approximately L4","100 cm; extends from the brain to the sacrum","45 cm; extends from the brain to approximately L2","45 cm; extends from the brain to approximately T12"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spinal cord is 45 cm in length and extends from the brain to approximately L2, passing through the foramen magnum. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What arteries supply the spinal cord, per the gross anatomy overview?","choices":["Four spinal arteries, two anterior and two posterior","One anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries","Only the vertebral arteries directly, with no dedicated spinal arteries","Two anterior spinal arteries and one posterior spinal artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spinal cord is fed by one anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the conus medullaris, and where does it typically end?","choices":["The true end of the spinal cord, typically ending at L1-L2","The beginning of the spinal cord at the foramen magnum","A bundle of nerve roots extending below the spinal cord's true end","A ligament connecting the spinal cord to the coccyx"],"answer":0,"exp":"The conus medullaris is the true end of the spinal cord, typically ending at L1-L2. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the cauda equina, and what does it innervate?","choices":["The enlarged region of the spinal cord found in the cervical area, housing upper limb motor neurons","The true, tapered end of the spinal cord itself, located at approximately L1-L2","Nerve roots extending from the conus medullaris to innervate pelvic and lower extremity structures","A thin ligament-like extension that becomes part of the coccygeal ligament inferiorly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cauda equina consists of nerve roots that extend from the conus medullaris to innervate pelvic and lower extremity structures. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the filum terminale, and what does it become?","choices":["A structure that becomes a component of the coccygeal ligament","The true, tapered end of the spinal cord, called the conus medullaris","A bundle of nerve roots innervating the lower extremities","The widened region of the spinal cord in the lumbosacral area"],"answer":0,"exp":"The filum terminale becomes a component of the coccygeal ligament. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the difference between the dorsal root and ventral root of a spinal nerve?","choices":["Dorsal root is motor, controlling limb muscles directly; ventral root is sensory, relaying pain and temperature","Dorsal root carries only autonomic fibers to visceral organs; ventral root carries only somatic sensory fibers","Dorsal root is sensory, with axons extending from the soma within the dorsal root ganglion; ventral root is motor","Dorsal root is motor, with cell bodies in the anterior gray horn; ventral root is sensory"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dorsal root is sensory, with axons extending from the soma within the dorsal root ganglion, while the ventral root is motor; the two merge to form a spinal nerve. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does the gray matter of the spinal cord consist of, and where is it located relative to the central canal?","choices":["Axons organized into tracts, located outside the gray matter area","Somas (cell bodies/nuclei) surrounding the central canal","Only glial cells, with no neurons","Only myelin, with no cell bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gray matter consists of somas (cell bodies; nuclei) surrounding the central canal, which supplies CSF. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the white matter of the spinal cord consist of, and how are its nerves organized?","choices":["Consists of axons, organized into tracts or columns (generally termed fascicles), located outside the gray matter","Consists of cerebrospinal fluid only, circulating freely within the central canal","Consists of neuron cell bodies and their dendrites, arranged surrounding the central canal region","Consists only of dorsal root ganglion tissue, located just outside the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"White matter consists of axons, organized into tracts or columns (generally termed fascicles), located outside the gray matter area. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do the posterior gray horns, lateral gray horns, and anterior gray horns each contain?","choices":["Posterior: visceral motor nuclei only; Lateral: somatic motor nuclei only; Anterior: somatic sensory nuclei only","Posterior: somatic motor nuclei only; Lateral: visceral sensory nuclei only; Anterior: somatic sensory nuclei only","Posterior: somatic motor nuclei; Lateral: somatic sensory nuclei; Anterior: visceral motor nuclei only","Posterior: somatic sensory/visceral nuclei; Lateral: visceral motor nuclei; Anterior: somatic motor nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior gray horns contain somatic sensory and visceral nuclei, the lateral gray horns contain visceral motor nuclei (present T1-L2, controlling smooth muscle, heart, and glands), and the anterior gray horns contain somatic motor nuclei. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In the anterior gray horn, how are extensor and flexor motor neurons arranged?","choices":["Extensors are more posterior/caudal; flexors are more anterior/rostral","Extensors are lateral; flexors are medial only","Extensors are more anterior/rostral; flexors are more posterior/caudal","Both are located in identical positions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Within the anterior gray horn, extensor motor neurons are positioned more anterior/rostral, while flexor motor neurons are positioned more posterior/caudal. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In the dorsal column sensory arrangement, how are the gracile and cuneate fasciculi organized medially/laterally, and what does each represent?","choices":["Gracilis is more lateral, responsible only for trunk sensation; cuneatus is more medial, responsible only for pelvic girdle sensation","Gracilis is more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity; cuneatus is more medial, responsible for the lower extremity and trunk","Both fasciculi occupy the identical medial-lateral position, together representing only trunk and axial body sensation","Gracilis is more medial, responsible for the lower extremity; cuneatus is more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity and trunk"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal column sensory material is arranged with the gracilis fasciculus more medial, responsible for the lower extremity, and the cuneatus fasciculus more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity and trunk (ceasing around T5-T6). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What arteries supply the spinal cord along its length, and what supplements them?","choices":["Only the basilar artery, arising from the union of the two vertebral arteries at the level of the pons, with no segmental supply","A single anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal arteries (from the vertebral arteries), supplemented by segmental branches mainly from the aorta","The pulmonary arteries, supplemented segmentally by branches of the internal jugular vein","Only the internal carotid arteries, with no segmental supplementation from the aorta"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vertebral arteries give off a single anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal arteries that descend along the cord, supplemented by segmental branches mainly arising from branches of the aorta. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where do veins of the spinal cord drain, and what venous systems do they ultimately connect to?","choices":["Drain into the pulmonary veins, ultimately connecting directly through to the left atrium of the heart itself","Drain into the hepatic portal vein, ultimately connecting to sinusoids within the liver","Drain directly into the internal jugular vein only, bypassing any vertebral venous plexuses","Drain into vertebral venous plexuses (e.g., internal venous plexus in the epidural space), ultimately connecting to the azygos and caval venous systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Veins of the spinal cord drain into several vertebral venous plexuses associated with the vertebral column, such as the internal venous plexus within the epidural space, ultimately connecting to the azygos and caval venous systems to reach the heart. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What role do microglia and white blood cells play in spinal cord meningeal lymphatics, per the material?","choices":["They are exclusively involved in CSF production, secreted by the choroid plexus ependymal cells","They play a role in protecting and healing the spinal cord from infections, injury, and immune-mediated disease, often following blood vessel patterning within the meninges","They have no known role in spinal cord immunity, functioning solely as structural support cells","They only function within the peripheral nervous system's Schwann cell sheaths and play no role at all within the spinal cord"],"answer":1,"exp":"Microglia and white blood cells play a role in protecting and healing the spinal cord from infections, injury, and immune-mediated diseases, often following the patterning of blood vessels within the meninges, though understanding of spinal cord meningeal lymphatics and immunity remains limited. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What sensory modalities do the dorsal columns carry?","choices":["Taste, smell, and general visceral sensation","Pain and temperature sensation only, throughout the entire body","Crude touch and pressure sensation only, throughout the body","Vibration, proprioception, and 2-point discrimination"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal columns carry vibration, proprioception, and 2-point discrimination (fine touch). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the fasciculus gracilis carry, and for which body region?","choices":["Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body only (T6 and up)","Crude touch and pain sensation only, for the entire body below the level of T7","Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the mid-thoracic to feet (T7 and down)","Pain and temperature sensation only, for the lower body below the level of T7"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fasciculus gracilis carries fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the mid-thoracic to feet region (T7 and inferiorly). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the fasciculus cuneatus carry, and for which body region?","choices":["Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception for the lower extremity only (T7 and down), excluding the trunk and the neck","Crude touch and pain sensation for the face and ears exclusively, bypassing the dorsal columns","Pain and temperature sensation only, for the upper body including the face and ears","Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body (T6 and up), including the upper extremity and neck but not the face/ears"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fasciculus cuneatus carries fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body (T6 and superior), including the upper extremity and neck, but not the face and ears. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where do the dorsal column tracts decussate, and what do they join after crossing?","choices":["Decussate in the spinal cord itself and join the spinothalamic tract","Decussate in the thalamus and join the corticospinal tract","Do not decussate at all","Decussate in the medulla and join the medial lemniscus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal column tracts ascend in the spinal cord, decussate in the medulla, and join the medial lemniscus, then enter the ventral nuclei of the thalamus before transmission to the primary sensory cortex. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What do the posterior (dorsal) and anterior (ventral) spinocerebellar tracts have in common?","choices":["Both are motor tracts descending directly from the primary motor cortex down to the lower motor neurons of the anterior horn","Both transmit conscious pain and temperature sensation to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus","Both transmit unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum, receiving input from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and pressure receptors","Both decussate together in the medulla's pyramids before ascending to the thalamus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both the posterior and anterior spinocerebellar tracts transmit unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum, receiving input from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and pressure receptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Where is the first-order neuron of the posterior (dorsal) spinocerebellar tract found, and how does it ascend to the cerebellum?","choices":["Found from C8 to S3; ascends ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar vermis cortex","Found only within the lumbar region (L1-L5); descends caudally back into the lower cord","Found from C3 to T7; ascends via the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus","Found from L1 to S2; decussates and ascends via the superior cerebellar peduncle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The first-order neuron of the posterior spinocerebellar tract is found from C8 to S3, ascending ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle and on to the cortex of the cerebellar vermis. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Where is the first-order neuron of the anterior (ventral) spinocerebellar tract found, and does it decussate?","choices":["Found from C8 down to S3; it ascends ipsilaterally without decussating at all, entering via the inferior cerebellar peduncle","Found in the cervical region only (C1-C7); decussates within the medulla oblongata before ascending","Found from C3 to T7; ascends via the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus in the medulla","Found from L1 to S2; decussates (contralateral) within the spinal cord in the anterior white commissure, entering the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The first-order neuron of the anterior spinocerebellar tract is found from L1 to S2, and it decussates (contralaterally) within the spinal cord in the anterior white commissure, entering the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the cuneocerebellar tract, and where is its first-order neuron found?","choices":["Transmits unconscious proprioceptive information for the upper extremity; first-order neuron found from C3 to T7, ascending within the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus","Transmits unconscious proprioceptive information for the lower extremity; the first-order neuron is found from C8 to S3, ascending ipsilaterally without decussation","A descending motor tract, not a sensory one, found only within the thoracic region from T1 to T7","Transmits conscious pain sensation for the lower extremity; first-order neuron found from L1 to S2, decussating centrally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cuneocerebellar tract transmits unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum for the entire upper extremity; its first-order neuron is found from C3 to T7, with axons ascending within the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus in the medulla, then a second-order neuron travels ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Where is the intermediolateral gray horn located, and what does it contain?","choices":["Prominent only within the midsacral region (S2-S4); it contains the parasympathetic preganglionic cells for the pelvic organs","Lies between the dorsal and ventral gray columns, prominent in the thoracic and upper lumbar regions; contains preganglionic cells for the autonomic nervous system","Lies entirely within the anterior white commissure; contains only somatic lower motor neurons","Lies entirely within the dorsal columns of white matter; contains only sensory neuron cell bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intermediolateral gray column (horn) lies between the dorsal and ventral gray columns, forming a prominent lateral triangular projection in the thoracic and upper lumbar regions (but not the midsacral region), and contains preganglionic cells for the autonomic nervous system. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Within spinal segments T1 to L2/L3, what pathway do preganglionic sympathetic axons take after leaving the intermediolateral gray column?","choices":["Remain entirely within the spinal cord's white matter tracts, never reaching the periphery at all","Travel via the gray rami communicantes directly to the paravertebral ganglia, bypassing the ventral root","Leave the spinal cord within the ventral roots, then travel to the sympathetic ganglia via the white rami communicantes","Leave the spinal cord within the dorsal roots, traveling directly to target organs without any ganglia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons within the intermediolateral gray column give rise to sympathetic axons that leave the spinal cord within the ventral roots, then travel to the sympathetic ganglia via the white rami communicantes. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the difference between white rami communicantes and gray rami communicantes?","choices":["White rami carry postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve; gray rami carry preganglionic fibers and exist at all spinal levels","White and gray rami are simply two names for the exact same structure at every spinal level","White rami exist only at T1-L2/L3 and carry preganglionic fibers to the paravertebral ganglia; gray rami carry postganglionic fibers","Both white and gray rami carry identical postganglionic fiber types at every spinal level"],"answer":2,"exp":"White rami communicantes exist only at spinal levels where the intermediolateral cell column is present (T1-L2/L3) and carry preganglionic nerve fibers from the spinal cord to the paravertebral ganglia, while gray rami communicantes carry postganglionic nerve fibers. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the lateral corticospinal tract innervate, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Innervates axial muscles; decussates at the level of the spinal cord itself","Innervates the diaphragm only; decussates within the thalamus before descending","Innervates limbs for skilled movements; decussates in the medulla's pyramids","Innervates limbs for skilled movements; does not decussate at any level"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lateral corticospinal tract innervates limbs for skilled movements, with its signal originating in the primary motor cortex and decussating in the medulla's pyramids; in the spinal cord, the tract is located laterally. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the anterior corticospinal tract innervate, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Innervates the axial muscles; does not decussate","Innervates the limbs; decussates in the thalamus","Innervates the axial muscles; decussates at the spinal cord level","Innervates limbs for skilled movements; decussates in the medulla's pyramids"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior corticospinal tract innervates the axial muscles, with its signal originating in the primary motor cortex, and decussation occurring at the spinal cord level; in the spinal cord, the tract is located anteriorly along the anterior median fissure. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What sensory information does the anterior spinothalamic tract carry, and where does it decussate?","choices":["Pain, pressure, and temperature sensation; it decussates within the medulla, several levels above the point of cord entry","Crude touch sensation; decussates within the thalamus itself, well above cord entry","Crude touch sensation; decussates in the spinal cord itself, through the anterior commissure, usually 1-2 levels above cord entry","Fine touch and proprioception; does not decussate at any spinal cord level"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior spinothalamic tract carries crude touch sensation, decussating in the spinal cord itself through the anterior commissure, usually 1-2 levels above the level the sensory fiber enters the cord. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What sensory information does the lateral spinothalamic tract carry?","choices":["Pain, pressure, and temperature sensation","Only pain sensation, with pressure and temperature carried elsewhere","Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception","Crude touch sensation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain, pressure, and temperature sensation, decussating in the spinal cord itself through the anterior commissure. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What pathway do the spinothalamic tracts follow from the posterior gray horn to the primary sensory cortex?","choices":["Enter the anterior gray horn directly, bypassing Lissauer's tract entirely, decussate within the thalamus itself, and then transmit back down to the medulla and sensory cortex","Enter the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract, decussate within the spinal cord, ascend to the medulla, transmit to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus, then to the primary sensory cortex","Enter the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract, then bypass the thalamus entirely, transmitting directly to the cortex","Enter the anterior gray horn, decussate in the medulla, and transmit directly to the cerebellum, bypassing the thalamus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sensory information enters the posterior gray horn through Lissauer's tract and decussates within the spinal cord itself, ascends to the medulla oblongata, transmits to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus, and is then transmitted to the primary sensory cortex. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 43"}]};
